Using 13 TeV proton-proton collision data, specifically 1.7 inverse femtobarns of luminosity collected by the LHCb detector, the shape of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate is determined as a function of hadron recoil. This rate is determined through the study of $D_s^{-}$ decays into $K^-K^+\pi^-$. The resulting shape is determined with relatively high precision, particularly at higher recoil values, and is found to be consistent with predictions made by several theoretical models that take into account various hadron effects. This finding is further confirmed by converting the $D_s^{-}$ decay rate into an equivalent $D_s^{*-}$ decay rate, with the $D_s^{*-}$ branching ratio set at 0.0531 ± 0.0061. This research adds valuable insights to existing $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ studies and highlights the significance of hadron recoil studies in heavy meson decays. Moreover, it facilitates more precise determinations of certain CKM matrix elements and selected rare charm processes. This analysis serves as a prime example of effective collision energy and strategy, with results that are anticipated to be surpassed by future LHCb data. This research was previously unavailable and represents one of the first differential decay rate measurements made for a heavy charm process that involves hadron recoil. It also introduces two new fit functions that describe the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rate as a function of hadron recoil systems. These fit functions are valuable tools for both experimental and theoretical researchers working on heavy hadron decay processes. This analysis was carried out as a part of the LHCb collaboration, with contributions made possible through grants from the UK Natural Environment Research Council, CEA and CNRS in France, the MSMT-LG 12034, GACR 13/37046M, 13/37047M, 13/37048M, 13B-130860, P304/12/7693, P302/12/7693, P302/12/7609, P302/12/7608, P302/12/7607, P302/12/7606, P302/12/7605, P302/12/7604, P302/
